\description{
\code{SlicePackageSet} aggregates multiple named \link{SlicePackage} objects (e.g.
several different oblique orientations, or several structures of
interest) into the single, final long-format table of points from which
intensity data are extracted for plotting via the Grammar of Graphics. A
bare \link{SliceGeometry} may be supplied anywhere a \code{SlicePackage} is expected
(in the constructor, \code{set_package()}, or \code{set_packages()}) and is
automatically wrapped as a single-slice (\code{size_k = 1}) package.

It also handles images with more than 3 dimensions (time, channel,
gradient direction, etc.): for each requested combination of non-spatial
indices, the corresponding 3D spatial sub-volume is extracted once (and
reused across every package that needs it) via \code{SimpleITK::Extract()},
before each package resamples it in a single call. Images sampled through
this class must therefore have at least 3 dimensions — every
\link{SliceGeometry}/\link{SlicePackage} is fundamentally a plane (or stack of parallel
planes) embedded in 3D physical space, so genuinely 1D/2D source images
are outside the scope of this sampling model.

Construct a \code{SlicePackageSet} containing the classic sagittal, coronal,
and axial single-slice planes through a reference image, at the given
coordinates.

Construct a \code{SlicePackageSet} from a plain (unnamed) list of
\link{SlicePackage} objects — or a single one, not wrapped in a list — rather
than a named list. Packages are auto-named \code{"package_1"}, \code{"package_2"},
etc., in the order given; use \code{set_packages()}/\code{rename_package()}
afterward for more meaningful names.
}
